{"example_index": 6006, "example_digest": "76f93a470df7f2fb153690c4db304fc66005814ba7fc52700edc5dd05dc5c13d", "completion": "Let Q(x) = (x+1)P(x) − x. Q has degree n+1 and roots 0, 1, …, n, so Q(x) = c·x(x−1)…(x−n).\n\nSetting x = −1 gives Q(−1) = 1, so c(−1)^{n+1}(n+1)! = 1, hence c = (−1)^{n+1}/(n+1)!.\n\nThen Q(n+1) = c·(n+1)! = (−1)^{n+1}, so (n+2)P(n+1) − (n+1) = (−1)^{n+1}.\n\n\\boxed{\\frac{n+1+(-1)^{n+1}}{n+2}}", "reference_completion": "The final answer is \\boxed{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1} + (n+1)}{n+2}}.", "source": "model", "judge": {"verdict": "correct", "reason": "The boxed answer (n+1+(-1)^{n+1})/(n+2) is algebraically identical to the ground truth.", "method": "claude"}}
{"example_index": 3181, "example_digest": "916e68c7260825d25c64ae01cff0129e0a100ad8749db522e7b7e28d65f59469", "completion": "Put the center of ω at the origin. Square ABCD has half-side 5√2.\n\nEach small square sits with one side on a side of ABCD. If its side is s, its outer corners are at (5√2+s, ±s/2) and lie on the circle:\n(5√2+s)² + s²/4 = 100.\nThis simplifies to s² + 8√2·s − 40 = 0, so s = 2√2.\n\nThe center of each small square is at distance 5√2 + s/2 = 6√2 from the origin. The sixth square therefore has diagonal 12√2.\n\nIts area is (12√2)²/2 = 144.\n\n\\boxed{144}", "reference_completion": "The final answer is \\boxed{144}.", "source": "model", "judge": {"verdict": "correct", "reason": "boxed answer equals the gold", "method": "strict"}}
{"example_index": 16456, "example_digest": "c6f8d59880bbea29a42390a57697b3c886e56ca3c9a64062540497a8a8a967f9", "completion": "Since the centroid is at the origin, |a|²+|b|²+|c|² equals one third of the sum of the squared side lengths.\n\nIn a right triangle the squared sides sum to 2h², by the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nSo 2h²/3 = 300, which gives h² = 450.\n\n\\boxed{450}", "reference_completion": "The final answer is \\boxed{450}.", "source": "model", "judge": {"verdict": "correct", "reason": "boxed answer equals the gold", "method": "strict"}}
{"example_index": 36771, "example_digest": "07cf63eb618822be4b2f71494fb72ec031a47a49208ab030d37a4f92ff07c998", "completion": "A sum of two nonnegative squares is 0 only when both terms are 0. So x = 0 and y = −5, the only point on the graph.\n\n\\boxed{-5}", "reference_completion": "The final answer is \\boxed{-5}.", "source": "model", "judge": {"verdict": "correct", "reason": "boxed answer equals the gold", "method": "strict"}}
{"example_index": 7695, "example_digest": "c3744c2699b877adfbb2e56c0a3d4ccd001a1f5de6cdbe329412b6433c0a0798", "completion": "The number of ways to choose k non-adjacent bulbs from 7 in a row is C(8−k, k).\n\n- k = 3: C(5,3) = 10\n- k = 4: C(4,4) = 1\n- k ≥ 5 is impossible.\n\nTotal: 10 + 1 = 11.\n\n\\boxed{11}", "reference_completion": "The final answer is \\boxed{11}.", "source": "model", "judge": {"verdict": "correct", "reason": "boxed answer equals the gold", "method": "strict"}}
{"example_index": 30515, "example_digest": "22674c3f691ec1f8cbc1977b7e3f78af76468c138a07e5c5d1681e0ae9246af4", "completion": "1. The circle has center (0,−2) and radius 5. A chord of length 8 means the line is at distance 3 from the center.\n   - The vertical line x = −3 is at distance 3, so it works.\n   - Otherwise, take y = k(x+3). Then |3k+2|/√(k²+1) = 3, which gives k = 5/12, i.e. 5x − 12y + 15 = 0.\n\n2. Subtracting the two circle equations gives the common chord x + 2y − 1 = 0. C₁ has center (−1,−4) and radius 5. Its distance to the chord is 10/√5 = 2√5. Half the chord is √(25−20) = √5, so the chord length is 2√5.\n\n3. The center lies on the perpendicular bisector x = −1 and on x − y − 2 = 0, so it is (−1,−3). The radius squared is 1 + 25 = 26, giving (x+1)² + (y+3)² = 26.\n\n4. PA·PB equals the power of P: (3−2)² + 4² − 1 = 16.\n\n\\boxed{1)\\ x=-3 \\text{ or } 5x-12y+15=0;\\ 2)\\ 2\\sqrt5;\\ 3)\\ (x+1)^2+(y+3)^2=26;\\ 4)\\ 16}", "reference_completion": "The final answer is \\boxed{16}.", "source": "model", "judge": {"verdict": "correct", "reason": "Part 4, which the ground truth targets, gives 16. That matches, and parts 1–3 are also correct.", "method": "claude"}}
{"example_index": 11727, "example_digest": "709f16701caac825604a2a7ce942c822724d96db31e6244a2433a7d461b8ee80", "completion": "Rationalize each term:\n- 2/(√5+2) = 2(√5−2)/(5−4) = 2√5 − 4\n- 3/(√7−2) = 3(√7+2)/(7−4) = √7 + 2\n\nTheir sum is 2√5 + √7 − 2.\n\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt5+\\sqrt7-2}}", "reference_completion": "The final answer is \\boxed{\\frac{2\\sqrt{5} + \\sqrt{7} + 2}{23 + 4\\sqrt{35}}}.", "source": "model", "judge": {"verdict": "correct", "reason": "Multiplying the denominator by its conjugate gives (2√5+√7−2)(2√5+√7+2) = 23+4√35. So 1/(2√5+√7−2) equals the ground-truth form.", "method": "claude"}}
{"example_index": 2443, "example_digest": "d1a1ff795307cafe9cbd0d4c95503f3b7d673490c81d7c58fc7835467d9c4144", "completion": "Let the numbers be x and y, with x + y = 4xy.\n\nThen 1/x + 1/y = (x + y)/(xy) = 4.\n\n\\boxed{4}", "reference_completion": "The final answer is \\boxed{4}.", "source": "model", "judge": {"verdict": "correct", "reason": "boxed answer equals the gold", "method": "strict"}}
